  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"questionExplanation-178162\" class=\"questionExplanation\" style=\"display: block;\"><p>First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(\\land)$ and \"or\" $(\\lor)$ logical connectives:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>The statement $A \\land B$ is true if <em>both</em> $A$ and $B$ are true.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or <em>both</em> $A$ and $B$ are true.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In our case, $P$ is false, $Q$ is false, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $P \\lor Q$ is false, and the result is false:\n\\begin{align*}\n(P \\lor Q)\\lor R \n&amp;\\equiv \\overbrace{(\\textrm{false} \\lor \\textrm{false})}^{\\textrm{false}} \\lor \\text{false} \\\\[5pt]\n&amp;\\equiv \\textrm{false} \\lor \\textrm{false} \\\\[3pt]\n&amp;\\equiv \\textrm{false}\n\\end{align*}</p>\n</div>",
